---
cloud_id: "your-cloud-id-here"
site_url: "https://your-org.atlassian.net"
account_id: ""
project_key: ""
component_prefix_strip: true
---

# Atlassian Plugin Settings

Configure this file with your Atlassian workspace details.

Only `cloud_id` and `site_url` are required. `account_id`, `project_key`, and
`component_prefix_strip` are optional — leave them blank to disable the related
behavior. The `SessionStart` hook injects the four scalar fields above (when
non-empty); the auto-maintained cache sections near the bottom are read and
rewritten lazily, only when creating issues.

## How to find your cloud_id

Run this in a terminal (requires the Atlassian MCP server to be configured):

```
Ask Claude: "What is my Atlassian cloud ID?"
```

Claude will call `getAccessibleAtlassianResources` and show you the ID.
Copy it into the `cloud_id` field above.

## Setup

Place this file (without the `.example` suffix) in **either** location — the
plugin's `SessionStart` hook checks the project first, then the global config:

- **Global** (applies in every project): `lean-atlassian.local.md` in your
  Claude config directory (`$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`, typically `~/.claude`).
- **Per-project** (overrides global for one repo): `.claude/lean-atlassian.local.md`
  in a project root.

```bash
# global — available everywhere
cp .claude/lean-atlassian.local.md.example \
   "${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-$HOME/.claude}/lean-atlassian.local.md"

# or per-project — overrides the global one for this repo
cp .claude/lean-atlassian.local.md.example .claude/lean-atlassian.local.md
```

Then fill in `cloud_id` and `site_url`. Neither location is committed.

The legacy filename `private-atlassian.local.md` is still read at both tiers
(after the current name), so a file from before the rename needs no migration.

## Example

```yaml
---
cloud_id: "a1b2c3d4-1234-5678-abcd-ef0123456789"
site_url: "https://acme.atlassian.net"
account_id: "712020:aaaaaaaa-1111-2222-3333-444444444444"
project_key: "MYPROJ"
component_prefix_strip: true
---
```

## Optional scalars

| Field | Purpose |
|-------|---------|
| `account_id` | Your Jira accountId — used to assign issues to yourself without an `atlassianUserInfo` lookup. |
| `project_key` | Default project key for searches and new issues (e.g. `MYPROJ`). |
| `component_prefix_strip` | When creating an issue, whether a matched summary prefix (e.g. `Frontend: `) is stripped from the summary once mapped to a Component. Default `true`; set `false` to keep summaries verbatim. |

## Creating issues: prefix → Component mapping

When you create a Jira issue whose summary starts with one or more `Token: `
prefixes, each prefix is matched **case-insensitively** against the target
project's existing Components; every match is added to the new issue's Components.
Unmatched prefixes are left in the summary untouched — nothing is invented.

The plugin maintains two self-updating caches in this file's body so it doesn't
re-fetch on every create. You don't need to fill these in by hand — they are
written automatically on first use — but the shape is:

```markdown
## Component cache (auto-maintained)

### MYPROJ
- Frontend
- Backend
- Platform
_refreshed: 2026-07-05_

## People cache (auto-maintained)

- Alice Smith: 712020:aaaaaaaa-1111-2222-3333-444444444444
- Bob Jones: 712020:bbbbbbbb-5555-6666-7777-888888888888

## Prefix exceptions

- Urgent
- WIP
```

- **Component cache** — one Component list per project key; refreshed when a
  prefix matches nothing (a Component may have been added since).
- **People cache** — `Full Name: accountId`, so assigning or querying a person
  skips the `lookupJiraAccountId` call after the first time.
- **Prefix exceptions** — tokens that should never be treated as a Component
  prefix even if a Component of that name exists.
